# Assemble mescc-tools-extra's bin directory.
#
# Everything above this bootstrap wants one directory to put on PATH rather
# than thirteen separate store paths, and this is where the utilities that
# have just been built make it.  mkdir creates it and cp fills it.
#
# The counterpart of
# ../../../linux/bootstrap/stage0-posix/mescc-tools-extra-bin.kaem, and shorter
# by a third: there is no chmod pass, because a PE32 file is runnable because
# of what is in its header and not because of a mode bit.  M2libc's Windows
# chmod accepts the call and does nothing, so the pass would not have failed --
# it would have been thirteen lies.
#
# The names end in .exe, which is what Windows expects of a program and what
# every executable this bootstrap produces is already called.  Nothing here
# depends on it: kaem is given the full path of what it runs.
#
# Every path comes from the environment, which is where `derivation` puts each
# attribute.  kaem has no loops, so the list is written out.

${bin_mkdir} -p ${out}/bin
${bin_cp} ${bin_sha256sum} ${out}/bin/sha256sum.exe
${bin_cp} ${bin_match} ${out}/bin/match.exe
${bin_cp} ${bin_mkdir} ${out}/bin/mkdir.exe
${bin_cp} ${bin_untar} ${out}/bin/untar.exe
${bin_cp} ${bin_ungz} ${out}/bin/ungz.exe
${bin_cp} ${bin_unbz2} ${out}/bin/unbz2.exe
${bin_cp} ${bin_unxz} ${out}/bin/unxz.exe
${bin_cp} ${bin_catm} ${out}/bin/catm.exe
${bin_cp} ${bin_cp} ${out}/bin/cp.exe
${bin_cp} ${bin_chmod} ${out}/bin/chmod.exe
${bin_cp} ${bin_rm} ${out}/bin/rm.exe
${bin_cp} ${bin_replace} ${out}/bin/replace.exe
${bin_cp} ${bin_wrap} ${out}/bin/wrap.exe
